        ans: 1,
        exp: "Rule: Under the modern common law rule established in United States v. Causby and Restatement (Second) of Torts § 159(2), flight by aircraft (including modern drones) in the airspace above the land of another constitutes a trespass if, but only if: (a) it enters into the immediate reaches of the airspace next to the land, and (b) it interferes substantially with the other's use and enjoyment of his land. Modern law has abandoned the ancient ad coelum doctrine (infinite upward ownership). Navigating a surveillance drone at a low altitude (150 feet) directly above an enclosed, private testing yard to capture concealed proprietary industrial data invades the immediate reaches of the airspace and substantially impairs the owner's use, enjoyment, and privacy, satisfying modern trespass standards (Option B). Option A is incorrect because FAA airspace authority does not grant blanket immunity from state common law trespass or privacy torts for low-altitude drone intrusions. Option C is incorrect because surface impact is not required for an aerial trespass within immediate reaches. Option D is incorrect because the infinite ad coelum doctrine was formally repudiated by Causby and the Restatement."
